Filetype: IR signals file
Version: 1
#
# Lasko Remote Control Ceramic Heater with Digital Display
# Model 755320
# https://laskoproducts.zendesk.com/hc/en-us/articles/19417783563533-Model-755320-Remote-Control-Ceramic-Heater-with-Digital-Display
#
# I was unable to find any good information on the CIR protocol Lasko uses for
# their heaters and fans. My rough guess as to what is going on is as follows:
#
# Features
# * 12-bit frames, 3 address bits, 7 command bits.
# * Symbol based modulation.
# * Carrier frequency of 38kHz.
# * Constant bit time of 1.6ms.
#
# Modulation
#
#       |<------[logical 1]------>| |<------[logical 0]------>|
#       |███████████████          | |          ███████████████|
#       |███████████████          | |          ███████████████|
# ▄▄▄▄▄ |███████████████▄▄▄▄▄▄▄▄▄▄| |▄▄▄▄▄▄▄▄▄▄███████████████| ▄▄▄▄▄
#       |<----1.25ms--->|<-0.4ms->| |<-0.4ms->|<----1.25ms--->|
#
# The protocol uses pulse width encoding, though both symbols come to the same
# overall length of 1.6ms. The symbol for a logical 1 is a 1.2ms burst followed
# by a 0.4ms gap. The symbol for a logical 0 is the reverse of this, so a 0.4ms
# gap followed by a 1.2ms burst.
#
# Protocol
#
#   |[ 1 ][ 1 ]|[ 0 ][ 1 ][ 1 ]|[ 0 ][ 0 ][ 0 ][ 0 ][ 0 ][ 0 ][ 1 ]|
#   |███  ███  |  ██████  ███  |  ███  ███  ███  ███  ███  ██████  |
# ▄▄|███▄▄███▄▄|▄▄██████▄▄███▄▄|▄▄███▄▄███▄▄███▄▄███▄▄███▄▄██████▄▄|▄▄
#   |          |MSB         LSB|MSB                             LSB|
#   |<-Header->|<-- Address -->|<------------ Command ------------>|
#
# The diagram shows a typical pulse train. The MSB is transmitted first. Frames
# begin with a 2-bit header, this probably acts something like the start burst
# in Sony's SIRC protocol. Then the 3-bit address is transmitted, followed by
# the 7-bit command. In this case address is 3 and command is 1.
#
# Hopefully this info is useful to the next poor soul that tries to mess with
# this.
#
name: Power
type: raw
